OLTP & OLAP
=============

OLTP: My name is Online Transaction Processing
OLAP: My name is Online Analytical Processing

OLTP: I work on individual entries 
OLAP: I can analyze large batches of data

OLTP: I can access recent data, from the last few hours or days
OLAP: I can access older data going back months, or even years 

OLTP: I can update the data also
OLAP: I can only read data

OLTP: I support fast real-time access 
OLAP: I support long running jobs 

OLTP: I work with single data source 
OLAP: I work with multiple data sources 

Data Lakehouse
===============

A data lakehouse combines the best of both data lakes & data warehouses.

Data lake benefits -
flexibility
cost-efficiency
scalability 
can hold all kinds of data

Data lake cannot offer -
ACID guarantees 
Schema enforcement

A Data lake can be used for ML kind of use cases but cannot serve a BI use case. 

A BI use case is better served by a Datawarehouse

Data warehouse benefits -
ACID guarantees
support schema enforcement

Data warehouse drawbacks -
proprietary format (ML not supported)

A Datawarehouse is used for BI kind of usecases but cannot serve ML use case because of proprietary file formats.

Earlier we used to have all our data in Data Lake & then a subset of this data used to go in Datawarehouse for serving BI use cases.

But the problem is that - 
we have to maintain 2 different systems
It is costly
A separate ETL activity is required

To solve this problem Databricks gave a paper on Lakehouse which proposed the solution by just having a single system that manages both the things.

Databricks have solved this using Delta Lake. They introduced metadata (transaction logs) on top of data lake which gives us datawarehouse like features.

Lakehouse is a concept and can have various implementations. Delta Lake is just one of these implementations.

Normalization and denormalization
====================================

Normalization vs Denormalization

Normalization is a process of dividing the data into multiple smaller tables with an intent to reduce data redundancy & inconsistency. 

However, Denormalization is totally opposite of above idea. 
Denormalization is the technique of combining the data into a big single table. This definitely leads to redundancy in the data.

Note: Redundancy causes inconsistency -
Consider that same data is repeated 2 times and when updating you update at one place and forget to do at second place. This leads to inconsistent state.

When retrieving data from Normalized tables we need to read many tables and perform join which is a costly operation.
However, when reading the data from denormalized tables it's quite fast as no joins are required.

When to use Normalized tables vs Denormalized ones?
when we talk about OLTP systems (Online Transaction Processing) where we deal with lot of insert, delete and updates then you should go for Normalized tables.

However, when you talk about OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) systems where you need to analyse historical data then Denormalized tables are best fit. 
Since you wont be doing updates on data here, even though after having redundancy we wont end up in inconsistent state.

Let's take a simple Example - when you make purchase on amazon then it requires a OLTP system (a rdbms kind of database). Here the tables should be normalized.

when amazon is doing data analysis of historical data, they will create denormalized tables just to make sure analysis is faster and costly joins can be avoided.

Data Lake
============

What is Delta Lake?

Before talking about Delta Lake we first need to understand Data Lake & its challenges.

Data Lake provides a very cost effective & scalable way to store any kind of data in form of raw files. 

Example of Data Lake is amazon s3, azure datalake gen2 etc.

The challenge with Data Lakes is that they do not provide ACID guarantees. (ACID properties are explained in my previous post)

1) There can be a job failure while overwriting existing data or appending new data. This will break Atomicity & Consistency guarantees.

2) Simultaneous read/write can also lead into inconsistent results as there is no Isolation.

3) Moreover there is no schema enforcement in Data Lake.

4) Also Data Lake does not offer updates & deletes.

5) we cannot maintain version history. 

and there are a few more limitations with Data Lake.
This is where Delta Lake comes for rescue..

So, what is Delta Lake?
it's nothing but a slight salt that we add along with parquet files.

what is this magic salt?
It is nothing but transaction logs stored in _delta_log (subfolder)

so delta format is nothing but a combination of:
parquet + transaction logs

Just by adding these transaction logs we are able to tackle all above limitations & we can get ACID features.

ACID
========

A stands for Atomicity 
C stands for Consistency
I stands for Isolation
D stands for Durability

but I could not relate things and understand this at all.


Let's assume you have a BOFA saving bank account & You have 25k in your account.

Minimum balance in account should be 5k

Consider, you are trying to transfer some amount to your friends account.

This involves 2 activities -
1. money deducted from your account
2. money added to your friends account

what if first activity succeeds and second one fail?

Money will be deducted from your account,
also your friend has not yet received the amount.

this will lead the system in a wrong/inconsistent state

1) Atomicity: It says either both the above activities happen as a whole or none of the activity happens. so that we never end up in an inconsistent state.

2) Consistency: lets say you want to transfer Rs 25000,
if the transaction goes through it will lead the system in an Inconsistent state as the balance will be 0 now,
and min balance should have been 5k

3) Isolation - you are transferring 10k to your friend
so first your account gets deducted - at time t1
later, your friend account get added with 10k - at time t3

what if a new reader tried to get your account balance and your friends account balance at time t2 which is in between t1 and t3?

If there is no isolation that person will see wrong data (money deducted from your account, but not added to your friends account)

A new reader should only see it this when both the above activities are done. This is called Isolation.

4) Durability means the committed changes should not be lost even in case of any failures.





